Chapter: EditBlockTest
  cites Works chapter GopEdit
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The Editor's state block leaves the desk's window slot alone, and the two
 buffers that were moved out of it still round-trip.

 Bytes 44 through 67 are the desk's: `dk-wnd-st-cell` at 44 and the window
 rectangle at 48 through 60, with 64 the first byte past them. The Editor kept
 four things in exactly that span -- the filename buffer at 44 to 55, the
 marks pointer at 56, the chapters pointer at 60 and the draft length at 64 --
 and that is the whole reason it was the one pane the windows campaign could
 not take. They live above 108 now and the block is 192 bytes.

 THE ZERO ROW IS THE ASSERTION AND THE OTHER TWO ARE ITS CONTROL. A block
 whose slot reads zero after a name and a draft have been written proves the
 span is nobody's; the round trips prove the move did not simply break the
 two buffers, which a zero row alone cannot tell you -- a chapter that stored
 nothing anywhere would pass the zero row perfectly.

 `ged-name-push` and `ged-name-of` had no test of any kind before this. That
 is what let the filename buffer sit unrecorded in the block map while two
 documents named only the marks and chapters pointers as the collision:
 nothing read those bytes except the Editor's own naming mode, and nothing
 tested the naming mode.

 SABOTAGED by putting `ged-name-off` back at 44, the zero row reads 5 and the
 two round trips do not move at all. That is the whole argument for having
 all three: the buffers work perfectly at either offset, and only the span
 census can see that one of them is sitting on the desk's rectangle.

 The block is 192 bytes here because `desk-run` allocates 192. A test that
 builds one of these builds it the size the desk builds it, for the reason the
 desk contract already gives for `ds`: the arena is bump-allocated and
 64-aligned, so a short block's over-read lands on padding and answers zero,
 which is indistinguishable from a cell that is legitimately empty.

Section: Test

 The characters are UNICODE code points in a list rather than a `Text`,
 because that is what the naming mode hands these two: a keycode, which
 `ged-name-push` uppercases and `ged-name-of` converts on the way back out.
 `annot-write` feeds the draft the same way for the same reason.

  eb-push-name : Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  eb-push-name (es) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then 0
    else let w = ged-name-push es (list-at xs i)
    in eb-push-name es xs (i + 1)

  eb-push-draft : Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  eb-push-draft (es) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then 0
    else let w = ged-draft-push es (list-at xs i)
    in eb-push-draft es xs (i + 1)

  eb-slot-nonzero : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  eb-slot-nonzero (es) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 68 then acc
    else eb-slot-nonzero es (i + 1) (if peek-byte es i /= 0 then acc + 1 else acc)

  eb-yn : Boolean -> Text
  eb-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let es = alloc-zeroed 192 64
    in let i0 = ged-init es
    in let nc = ged-name-clear es
    in let dc = ged-draft-clear es
    in let np = eb-push-name es [110, 111, 116, 101, 115] 0
    in let dp = eb-push-draft es [97, 32, 100, 114, 97, 102, 116] 0
    in let nonzero = eb-slot-nonzero es 44 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("name round trip  : " & ged-name-of es)
      print-line-uni ("draft round trip : " & ged-draft-of es)
      print-line-uni ("marks and chaps set: " & eb-yn (ged-marks es /= 0 & ged-chaps es /= 0))
      print-line-uni ("bytes 44..67 nonzero: " & integer-to-text nonzero)
    end

